  "worked_solution": "First, let's approximate the number of orange and peach trees by rounding to the nearest $100$:\n\n- Orange trees: $352⟶400$\n\n- Peach trees: $618⟶600$\n\nThus, the total number of trees is approximately $400+600=1\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of orange trees to the total number of trees is approximately:\n\n$$\n\\frac{400}{1\\,000}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{400÷100}{1\\,000÷100}=\\frac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of orange trees to the total number of trees is approximately $\\frac{2}{5}.$",
